{
  "an9.36:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 9.36 ",
  "an9.36:0.2": "4. The Great Chapter ",
  "an9.36:0.3": "Depending on Absorption ",
  "an9.36:1.1": "“Mendicants, I say that the first absorption is a basis for ending the defilements. ",
  "an9.36:1.2": "The second absorption is also a basis for ending the defilements. ",
  "an9.36:1.3": "The third absorption is also a basis for ending the defilements. ",
  "an9.36:1.4": "The fourth absorption is also a basis for ending the defilements. ",
  "an9.36:1.5": "The dimension of infinite space is also a basis for ending the defilements. ",
  "an9.36:1.6": "The dimension of infinite consciousness is also a basis for ending the defilements. ",
  "an9.36:1.7": "The dimension of nothingness is also a basis for ending the defilements. ",
  "an9.36:1.8": "The dimension of neither perception nor non-perception is also a basis for ending the defilements. ",
  "an9.36:1.9": "The cessation of perception and feeling is also a basis for ending the defilements. ",
  "an9.36:2.1": "‘The first absorption is a basis for ending the defilements.’ ",
  "an9.36:2.2": "That’s what I said, but why did I say it? ",
  "an9.36:2.3": "Take a mendicant who, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption. ",
  "an9.36:2.4": "They contemplate the phenomena there—included in form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness—as impermanent, as suffering, as diseased, as a boil, as a dart, as gloom, as an afflictio